## Conventions

- Playbooks are lists of roles. Logic lives in roles. One playbook per host type, imported from `playbooks/site.yml`.
- Fully qualified module names (`ansible.builtin.copy`). Every task and play has a capitalized `name`.
- Role variables are prefixed with the role name. Tunables live in `defaults/main.yml`, internal values in `vars/main.yml`.
- Prefer a module over `command` or `shell`. A `command` task needs `creates`, `removes` or `changed_when`.
- Every role must pass `make test`, including the idempotence check, and work under `--check`.
- Inventory is a directory per environment. Always pass `-i inventories/<env>`.
- Commits follow `.github/commit_template.md`. Fill out `.github/pull_request_template.md`, including the rollback plan.

## Testing and validation

Before finishing a change, `make lint` and `make syntax` must pass, and `make test` must pass for each changed role. Fix the cause of a finding. Do not add a rule to `skip_list` to get a change through.

## Boundaries

- Do not run `ansible-playbook`, `make check`, `make deploy` or any apply workflow unless the user explicitly asks. Production needs a recorded change plan first.
- For production, always check first (`make check`), review the diff, then apply with the user's confirmation. Never skip the check.
- Ask before changing `inventories/production/` or production playbooks.
- Tasks that write secrets into files set `no_log: true` and `diff: false`.
- Never decrypt, view or print vault files, and never read `~/.vault/`. Never put a secret in plain text in any file, log, commit or chat. Stop and tell the user if you find one.
- Do not add users, keys or access methods without explicit approval.
- Do not store PII in this repository.
- Ask for clarification when a missing decision blocks safe progress instead of guessing.
